WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.820
Does a pea hand on its genes one at a time, or in bundles?

2
00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:10.940
Chapter 1 followed one gene, for seed shape.

3
00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:13.920
Now add a second, for seed colour.

4
00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:20.183
Mendel crossed a line with round yellow seeds to a line with wrinkled green seeds.

5
00:00:21.403 --> 00:00:23.483
Every hybrid seed was round and yellow.

6
00:00:24.567 --> 00:00:26.787
So the hybrid holds two pairs of cards.

7
00:00:27.987 --> 00:00:30.307
A shape pair, one round and one wrinkled.

8
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:33.687
A colour pair, one yellow and one green.

9
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:39.500
When it makes an egg or pollen grain, does it toss the two pairs together, or

10
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.500
separately?

11
00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:42.983
Suppose they were glued together.

12
00:00:44.503 --> 00:00:47.963
Round and yellow came in from one parent, so they would leave together.

13
00:00:49.183 --> 00:00:51.583
The hybrid would make only two kinds of gamete.

14
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:54.827
Now suppose each pair has its own coin.

15
00:00:56.167 --> 00:00:58.587
Any shape card can go with any colour card.

16
00:00:59.667 --> 00:01:02.707
The hybrid would make four kinds of gamete, a quarter each.

17
00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:08.550
A selfed seed mixes two of the hybrid's gametes, and dominance hides some copies.

18
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:12.190
So how could you read the hybrid's gametes directly?

19
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:21.487
Cross the hybrid to a wrinkled green plant.

20
00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:25.667
That tester can only give wrinkled and green copies.

21
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:29.867
So each seed shows which copies the hybrid gave it.

22
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:34.620
Mendel ran this cross with the hybrid as the seed parent.

23
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.240
The seeds fell into four kinds, in roughly equal numbers.

24
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:45.810
Then he ran it the other way, with the hybrid as the pollen parent.

25
00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.130
Again, four kinds, roughly equal.

26
00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:54.820
So eggs and pollen alike carried all four combinations about equally.

27
00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:57.960
Fully glued pairs cannot do that.

28
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:00.360
Two separate coins can.

29
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:05.243
Now let the double hybrid pollinate itself, as Mendel did.

30
00:02:06.523 --> 00:02:08.803
Separate coins predict how its seeds should split.

31
00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:11.627
Draw a square for all the seeds.

32
00:02:13.127 --> 00:02:16.627
Shape cuts it into three quarters round and one quarter wrinkled.

33
00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:19.247
Colour cuts it the other way.

34
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.440
Separate coins multiply, so each box's share is a product.

35
00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.540
The round yellow corner is the biggest.

36
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.740
The wrinkled green corner is the smallest.

37
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:34.063
Mendel's counts sat close to those shares.

38
00:02:35.683 --> 00:02:38.523
Close, but not exact, as real counts usually are.

39
00:02:39.517 --> 00:02:41.897
This held for seed shape and seed colour.

40
00:02:43.097 --> 00:02:45.117
It does not hold for every pair of genes.

41
00:02:46.497 --> 00:02:48.097
Why not comes in a later lesson.

42
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.860
So here is the whole idea.

43
00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.960
Two copies of each gene, and one goes to each gamete by a coin toss.

44
00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:01.640
Genes that assort independently are separate coins, like seed shape and colour.
